<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Convention of Kanagawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1854</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Convention of Kanagawa was the treaty signed in 1854 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a>'s expedition. It opened two ports to American vessels and ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s policy of national seclusion. The treaty exposed the military weakness of the Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">Shogunate</a> and helped bring about its downfall.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/convention-of-kanagawa.jpg" alt="Convention of Kanagawa" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ratification_of_the_Japan_USA_Treaty_of_Peace_and_Amity_21_February_1855.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
